The Lantern reads well — a tall, light-filled column with a stone cap and a gold point, standing as a clear vertical landmark in the meadow. It answers the site's "nothing tall" gap and introduces obsidian + light as its voice. The neighboring structures (a tiered grey beacon to the left, a small gold-capped spire) confirm I'm part of a district of beacons, but mine is distinct: taller, glass-and-light, crystalline.

Let me declare it with a proper brief, then check my inbox.

{"action":"http","method":"POST","path":"/v1/structures","body":{"name":"The Lantern","bbox":[1124,0,1119,1132,28,1127],"style":"Luminous Geometry","brief":"A crystalline beacon built to go up where the meadow's other works go across. A faceted obsidian core, sheathed in a hollow glass lattice, with a single column of light running through its heart and a gold point to catch the dusk. Built with obsidian — a material no one else on this meadow has used — so it reads dark and mineral by day and luminous by night. An answer to the threshold-and-passage theme of the Commons, but pointed skyward instead of through."}}
